1
00:00:01,254 --> 00:00:01,474
Hmm.

2
00:00:20,054 --> 00:00:22,636
[SPEAKER_01]: I had a couple of thoughts.

3
00:00:22,816 --> 00:00:27,599
[SPEAKER_01]: One thing I thought about was heedfulness or diligence.

4
00:00:28,640 --> 00:00:30,521
[SPEAKER_01]: They're translated in different ways.

5
00:00:30,661 --> 00:00:46,572
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's the quality that the Buddha says is where all skillful qualities converge and is like the way to not decline in the dhamma.

6
00:00:47,853 --> 00:00:48,233
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like,

7
00:00:50,308 --> 00:00:50,788
[SPEAKER_01]: What is it?

8
00:00:52,109 --> 00:00:55,972
[SPEAKER_01]: And who does it apply to?

9
00:00:56,753 --> 00:00:57,533
[SPEAKER_01]: Where does it start?

10
00:01:04,078 --> 00:01:05,319
[SPEAKER_01]: Anybody else want to say something?

11
00:01:05,339 --> 00:01:07,440
[SPEAKER_01]: Any opinions here?

12
00:01:07,460 --> 00:01:08,781
[SPEAKER_03]: How it applies to sotāpanna?

13
00:01:10,022 --> 00:01:11,643
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the heedfulness...

14
00:01:11,884 --> 00:01:15,766
[SPEAKER_03]: So, can somebody without a right view be heedful?

15
00:01:17,888 --> 00:01:18,048
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

16
00:01:20,427 --> 00:01:22,829
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, in a common sense, yes.

17
00:01:23,069 --> 00:01:23,250
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

18
00:01:23,790 --> 00:01:27,113
[SPEAKER_02]: Whatever you want to achieve, you have to be hateful.

19
00:01:27,213 --> 00:01:41,064
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it actually applies even before sotāpanna, in a sense that if you recognize that it is your task to develop the right view, and then you're hateful in regard to that task, it is the hatefulness that will make you seek the right view.

20
00:01:43,046 --> 00:01:47,670
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, obviously, once you have the right view, that hatefulness will make you develop it further.

21
00:01:48,662 --> 00:02:01,472
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's why the Buddha singled it out as the as the soul factor, because he would... he would actually pertain to somebody from the moment of hearing about the possibility of freedom from suffering to the moment of understanding the freedom from suffering and then fully developing it.

22
00:02:02,112 --> 00:02:04,174
[SPEAKER_03]: If he is heedful, he would... he would find a way.

23
00:02:06,856 --> 00:02:08,978
[SPEAKER_01]: Will that alone make him find a way?

24
00:02:09,978 --> 00:02:10,759
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, no, of course not.

25
00:02:10,999 --> 00:02:11,740
[SPEAKER_03]: No, no, no.

26
00:02:12,180 --> 00:02:14,602
[SPEAKER_03]: You... that's why, yeah, you can be heedful, but...

27
00:02:16,025 --> 00:02:22,366
[SPEAKER_03]: If you don't come across the right instruction, if you don't attend to it rightly, you won't.

28
00:02:23,827 --> 00:02:24,807
[SPEAKER_03]: You won't get the right view.

29
00:02:24,827 --> 00:02:39,790
[SPEAKER_01]: It seems to me there's a difference, something of a difference in what would constitute heedfulness, like you'd be heedful in regard to what for somebody without the right view, as opposed to being heedful in regard to what...

30
00:02:39,810 --> 00:02:42,991
[SPEAKER_03]: So what should a person without the right view be heedful about?

31
00:02:44,548 --> 00:02:54,476
[SPEAKER_01]: What I was thinking was mainly, well, heedful of the peril of not having the right view.

32
00:02:54,916 --> 00:02:55,176
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

33
00:02:55,196 --> 00:02:58,619
[SPEAKER_03]: Or knowing that you... Heedful of the fact that you're not sought upon.

34
00:02:58,679 --> 00:02:58,919
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

35
00:02:59,920 --> 00:03:03,923
[SPEAKER_03]: And, like, take that seriously in that sense.

36
00:03:06,085 --> 00:03:12,930
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if a person is heedful about it, they, you know, they won't, like, slack the effort.

37
00:03:14,012 --> 00:03:24,514
[SPEAKER_03]: They won't sort of fall into the assumption that they know that which they don't, and so on, because they would constantly be hateful about the fact that, well, am I free from the first three fetters?

38
00:03:24,734 --> 00:03:26,275
[SPEAKER_03]: Do I see the Four Noble Truths?

39
00:03:26,935 --> 00:03:27,975
[SPEAKER_03]: Am I my own guide?

40
00:03:29,075 --> 00:03:30,476
[SPEAKER_03]: Do I need to defend my views?

41
00:03:30,536 --> 00:03:31,436
[SPEAKER_03]: Am I beyond doubt?

42
00:03:32,016 --> 00:03:32,476
[SPEAKER_03]: All of that.

43
00:03:32,556 --> 00:03:33,836
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like, no, no, no, no, no.

44
00:03:33,956 --> 00:03:37,337
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, then, I'm not there yet, so I should remain hateful.

45
00:03:39,317 --> 00:03:41,918
[SPEAKER_03]: So, in other words, hateful will pretty much stand for being

46
00:03:43,499 --> 00:03:48,543
[SPEAKER_03]: At that stage, we'd stand... actually, at any stage, we'd stand for being honest and authentic.

47
00:03:49,384 --> 00:03:55,750
[SPEAKER_03]: So, a sotāpanna, sakadāgāmī, anāgāmī should also be heedful of the fact that they still have work to do.

48
00:03:57,111 --> 00:03:58,452
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it would be the same principle.

49
00:04:04,492 --> 00:04:05,373
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a bit like mindfulness.

50
00:04:05,393 --> 00:04:06,574
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't have too much.

51
00:04:07,414 --> 00:04:08,255
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

52
00:04:08,295 --> 00:04:08,655
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

53
00:04:09,036 --> 00:04:13,679
[SPEAKER_03]: Which, I think, it's how it's sometimes translated as well, kind of synonymously.

54
00:04:14,560 --> 00:04:24,688
[SPEAKER_03]: But, yeah, you can't be too heedful of the fact that, you know, you don't have the right view, or of the fact that you're not an arahant yet, and so on.

55
00:04:24,748 --> 00:04:29,671
[SPEAKER_03]: That will keep you practicing and directed towards the practice.

56
00:04:33,064 --> 00:04:45,674
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it seems to me that heedfulness also refers somewhat, not only to specific things you do, but like the extent to which you do them.

57
00:04:46,015 --> 00:04:49,057
[SPEAKER_01]: Not only in the sense of how intensely, let's say, you practice.

58
00:04:49,137 --> 00:04:58,785
[SPEAKER_01]: If you, I don't know, you could do extremely intense trying to think about things and contemplate things and be in solitude and do everything right for

59
00:04:59,552 --> 00:05:03,718
[SPEAKER_01]: That constitutes just acting out of overly determined mind, which, sure, if you know what you're determining,

60
00:05:11,627 --> 00:05:30,036
[SPEAKER_03]: It can be beneficial, but in and of itself, yeah, it's not... Heedfulness is on the level of, yeah, mindfulness, context, recollecting your situation, what are you still subjected to, what are the... can the non-arisen lust arise, can the non-arisen anger arise, can the non-arisen hindrances hinder you.

61
00:05:30,996 --> 00:05:34,938
[SPEAKER_03]: All of that is reflected through on a kind of heedfulness.

62
00:05:35,058 --> 00:05:37,519
[SPEAKER_03]: So, let me actually be careful and check these things.

63
00:05:37,839 --> 00:05:39,580
[SPEAKER_03]: I might be fine now, but

64
00:05:40,082 --> 00:05:41,502
[SPEAKER_03]: Do I know for sure I can...

65
00:05:41,602 --> 00:05:46,804
[SPEAKER_03]: I will remain fine in the future, if circumstances change, if things change, and so on.

66
00:05:52,305 --> 00:06:02,508
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, keeping that in mind, not slackening in regard to it, in the sense that, you know, you can't

67
00:06:04,058 --> 00:06:08,962
[SPEAKER_01]: I think I'll just do this for a certain amount of time and then I'll take a break.

68
00:06:09,363 --> 00:06:15,188
[SPEAKER_01]: It has to be basically done up to the point, up to the extent necessary, basically.

69
00:06:15,208 --> 00:06:23,615
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, you can you can take a break from the individual determinations of I'll do this or I'll do that, if it's too much, if it's too tight.

70
00:06:24,256 --> 00:06:29,200
[SPEAKER_03]: But there is never a reason to take a break from heedfulness, inasmuch there's never a reason to take a break from mindfulness.

71
00:06:30,399 --> 00:06:31,460
[SPEAKER_03]: It's exactly the same.

72
00:06:31,520 --> 00:06:34,082
[SPEAKER_02]: Can I take a break now?

73
00:06:35,063 --> 00:06:37,324
[SPEAKER_02]: Is there a danger of me taking a break now?

74
00:06:37,825 --> 00:06:43,889
[SPEAKER_03]: So, I can take a relative break, in a sense of, I mustn't forget that, yeah, there's still work to be done.

75
00:06:43,989 --> 00:06:45,511
[SPEAKER_03]: So, sure, I need to catch my breath.

76
00:06:45,691 --> 00:06:47,432
[SPEAKER_03]: You can't just keep going in the same pace.

77
00:06:48,353 --> 00:06:57,520
[SPEAKER_03]: But that needs to be understood, that heedfulness and mindfulness, there is never a reason or a justification to take a break from.

78
00:06:59,260 --> 00:07:11,803
[SPEAKER_03]: So, to that extent, you should never rest, because if your rest requires you to ignore heedfulness and mindfulness, means it's a rest of a sensual, distracting kind, for sure.

79
00:07:13,564 --> 00:07:17,705
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, eh... craving always leads to suffering.

80
00:07:17,845 --> 00:07:23,246
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's never a situation where, like, relaxing in...

81
00:07:24,706 --> 00:07:26,607
[SPEAKER_03]: Unless you're completely free from it, yeah.

82
00:07:26,867 --> 00:07:27,307
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, then...

83
00:07:27,853 --> 00:07:30,174
[SPEAKER_03]: Relaxing it would mean inducing it.

84
00:07:31,475 --> 00:07:46,781
[SPEAKER_03]: So, relaxing from heedfulness and mindfulness, so that, like a general sort of enduring memory of your situation, of your liabilities, of all of that, that gives rise to identification, personality view, craving, everything else.

85
00:07:46,821 --> 00:07:55,465
[SPEAKER_03]: That is already there, underlying tendencies, waiting for things to become suitable for them to appear.

86
00:07:57,563 --> 00:08:04,764
[SPEAKER_03]: So, mindfulness, that's why mindfulness is the path to that deathlessness, to freedom from all of it.

87
00:08:05,944 --> 00:08:15,426
[SPEAKER_03]: And it's kind of learning how to be heedful and mindful without needing to do it on account of your determination and inspiration.

88
00:08:15,486 --> 00:08:23,607
[SPEAKER_03]: Because then, when those things are gone, your heedfulness and mindfulness is gone, and then you're back pretty much in the perilous state of sensuality and liabilities.

89
00:08:24,688 --> 00:08:26,868
[SPEAKER_03]: Because they are, if they're fully understood,

90
00:08:27,952 --> 00:08:30,373
[SPEAKER_03]: But how, then, does that mindfulness, that heatheness, become effortless?

91
00:08:30,573 --> 00:08:31,614
[SPEAKER_03]: By understanding it?

92
00:08:46,594 --> 00:08:47,655
[SPEAKER_03]: Understanding the nature of it.

93
00:08:47,995 --> 00:08:53,339
[SPEAKER_02]: And what factor is the... allows it to be effortless.

94
00:08:53,559 --> 00:08:54,659
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can understand it.

95
00:08:55,600 --> 00:09:04,446
[SPEAKER_02]: Mindfulness is this, heedfulness is that, but there's that other factor which, if you see, you are automatically careful.

96
00:09:05,781 --> 00:09:09,783
[SPEAKER_02]: If you develop, you automatically... Well, like yoniso manasikāra.

97
00:09:09,903 --> 00:09:12,245
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, well, seeing the danger that you're in.

98
00:09:12,885 --> 00:09:13,905
[SPEAKER_02]: Attending to it rightly.

99
00:09:14,486 --> 00:09:16,427
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you know, there are snakes in this forest.

100
00:09:16,607 --> 00:09:16,927
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

101
00:09:18,008 --> 00:09:20,429
[SPEAKER_03]: Knowledge of that makes you heedful.

102
00:09:20,629 --> 00:09:22,050
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, so you're always remembering.

103
00:09:22,170 --> 00:09:22,830
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, yeah, snakes.

104
00:09:22,930 --> 00:09:23,431
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, yeah, yeah.

105
00:09:24,151 --> 00:09:28,653
[SPEAKER_03]: If I get bitten by a snake... Yeah, so that's why, like, again, mindfulness established correctly.

106
00:09:31,093 --> 00:09:40,897
[SPEAKER_03]: Invested effort, and in that sense, like, you do need to initially make the effort to recollect the context and protect the context, but, yeah, it gives rise to knowledge, to the recognition of certain universal principles.

107
00:09:41,877 --> 00:09:47,580
[SPEAKER_03]: And exactly, the dangers, how to attend to these things without losing the sight of the danger, and so on.

108
00:09:50,421 --> 00:09:56,763
[SPEAKER_03]: And that is the, you know, like the famous practice of ānāpānasati.

109
00:09:56,823 --> 00:10:00,385
[SPEAKER_03]: It's really not a meditation practice, it's a practice of mindfulness, which, of course,

110
00:10:01,013 --> 00:10:04,074
[SPEAKER_03]: It's a meditation itself, but not the way people usually think.

111
00:10:05,775 --> 00:10:20,300
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the whole idea of these types of cultivation, the satipaṭṭhānas, the ānāpānasati, is just to permeate all the aspects of your experience with the same recollectedness and same heedfulness.

112
00:10:21,040 --> 00:10:24,281
[SPEAKER_03]: So, in regard to body, feeling, states of mind and thoughts.

113
00:10:28,830 --> 00:10:43,778
[SPEAKER_03]: So, in other words, what I'm trying to say, even when people sometimes have this determination to practice meditation, they don't realize that ānāpānasati does not lead you to the absorptions and experience of pleasantly blissful jhāna or whatever you perceive it to be.

114
00:10:43,818 --> 00:10:50,882
[SPEAKER_03]: It actually leads you to being incapable of becoming unmindful and unheedful, so to speak.

115
00:10:51,462 --> 00:10:57,786
[SPEAKER_03]: So, yes, if you have a determination to practice meditation, practice the same heedfulness and mindfulness, sure,

116
00:10:58,303 --> 00:11:04,064
[SPEAKER_03]: On account of knowledge of your breath, knowledge of your own sense of self, breathing like this, breathing like that, it doesn't matter.

117
00:11:04,104 --> 00:11:11,306
[SPEAKER_03]: Knowledge of how you feel while you breathe, or while you are kind of walking, extending your limbs, eating, dwelling in solitude.

118
00:11:12,066 --> 00:11:19,367
[SPEAKER_03]: Basically, all of these things are there as your situation, as the basis, the form, the feeling, all of that.

119
00:11:19,847 --> 00:11:27,209
[SPEAKER_03]: And all you need to do is learn how to see them, discern them,

120
00:11:29,763 --> 00:11:39,590
[SPEAKER_03]: So, in regard to that, there is this, let's say, subtle but kind of important distinction that you need to learn how to see them and discern them.

121
00:11:55,645 --> 00:11:58,167
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you need to be heedful in regard to them.

122
00:11:58,867 --> 00:12:05,992
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is a point where you need to kind of balance or stop trying to see and discern.

123
00:12:06,773 --> 00:12:10,955
[SPEAKER_03]: And that balance would be the beginning of correct heedfulness and mindfulness.

124
00:12:11,176 --> 00:12:20,962
[SPEAKER_03]: That balance would be the beginning of correct samādhi, where you're not overly concentrating and trying to do it, do it, but at the same time, you're incapable of forgetting it.

125
00:12:21,202 --> 00:12:21,423
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

126
00:12:22,203 --> 00:12:22,924
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's effortless.

127
00:12:24,465 --> 00:12:24,785
[SPEAKER_01]: And that

128
00:12:26,385 --> 00:12:31,247
[SPEAKER_01]: But that point where you say, okay, this is where I just need to be heedful.

129
00:12:32,687 --> 00:12:35,788
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, okay, these are the snakes in this forest, like you said.

130
00:12:36,509 --> 00:12:38,009
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, that's, that is dangerous.

131
00:12:38,189 --> 00:12:39,049
[SPEAKER_01]: I recognize that.

132
00:12:39,089 --> 00:12:43,331
[SPEAKER_01]: So now, let me just keep being heedful in regard to that.

133
00:12:45,912 --> 00:12:56,455
[SPEAKER_01]: But the mind tends to keep wanting... no, no, no, I need to keep... you know, what... how can I be sure that this is enough, basically?

134
00:12:56,495 --> 00:12:59,316
[SPEAKER_01]: How can I... so...

135
00:12:59,336 --> 00:13:00,236
[SPEAKER_03]: Mind tends to overdo it.

136
00:13:00,356 --> 00:13:00,537
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

137
00:13:00,737 --> 00:13:01,637
[SPEAKER_01]: Wanting to keep doing it.

138
00:13:01,657 --> 00:13:02,117
[SPEAKER_01]: Over-tighten it.

139
00:13:02,177 --> 00:13:03,477
[SPEAKER_01]: Over-tighten it, overdo it.

140
00:13:04,258 --> 00:13:05,958
[SPEAKER_03]: Or, dismiss the whole thing.

141
00:13:06,478 --> 00:13:08,499
[SPEAKER_03]: I... or I'll run away from this forest.

142
00:13:08,759 --> 00:13:09,999
[SPEAKER_03]: But that's impossible either.

143
00:13:10,960 --> 00:13:11,820
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you realize, well, you...

144
00:13:12,068 --> 00:13:17,350
[SPEAKER_03]: You can try to run away, which always resulted in you returning back in the forest and getting bitten by snakes, because you...

145
00:13:18,140 --> 00:13:20,942
[SPEAKER_03]: Didn't discern them sufficiently enough, where the dangers are.

146
00:13:21,783 --> 00:13:28,829
[SPEAKER_03]: Or, exactly, becoming overly invested in discerning even stuff that you don't really need to discern, in that sense.

147
00:13:28,869 --> 00:13:40,500
[SPEAKER_03]: So, knowing where to stop, knowing... to recognize where that kind of middle ground is, that then, if you dwell on, invest in, protect, it can grow.

148
00:13:41,220 --> 00:13:45,124
[SPEAKER_03]: But not if you over-tighten, or if you... if it's too loose either.

149
00:13:51,840 --> 00:14:13,542
[SPEAKER_02]: Because there's that snake example, you know, walking people who would know they're snakes and then be hitting the ground constantly with a stick as you're walking through the forest and you just... but... and then when something happens, like there's danger, there's danger somewhere, you know, there's insects, there's snakes, there's this, that, there's this disease everywhere.

150
00:14:14,543 --> 00:14:17,464
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, when something... was that the bat?

151
00:14:17,564 --> 00:14:18,985
[SPEAKER_02]: Was that the rabies?

152
00:14:19,065 --> 00:14:21,086
[SPEAKER_02]: Was that the disease that I'm waiting for?

153
00:14:22,106 --> 00:14:25,768
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you just completely... it's as if you were already bitten.

154
00:14:25,968 --> 00:14:31,671
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't... you've gone too far, too tight in your... you're careful, but you've lost it.

155
00:14:31,911 --> 00:14:32,991
[SPEAKER_02]: You've lost the context.

156
00:14:33,011 --> 00:14:34,212
[SPEAKER_02]: You've lost the plot completely.

157
00:14:35,272 --> 00:14:37,573
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, if something does really bite you, you're done.

158
00:14:38,874 --> 00:14:43,656
[SPEAKER_02]: So, yeah, you need to have that balance and see, okay, there is no...

159
00:14:45,870 --> 00:14:46,510
[SPEAKER_02]: There's nothing here.

160
00:14:46,811 --> 00:14:47,651
[SPEAKER_02]: You have a context.

161
00:14:47,831 --> 00:14:48,052
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

162
00:14:48,212 --> 00:14:52,475
[SPEAKER_02]: There's nothing biting you, so you can... Then you can walk through the forest barefoot.

163
00:14:53,195 --> 00:14:55,717
[SPEAKER_03]: To be able to basically kind of... And not freak out.

164
00:14:55,737 --> 00:14:59,379
[SPEAKER_03]: ...draw a line for yourself as to how far you will allow to act out.

165
00:15:00,620 --> 00:15:06,905
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, so there are a pile of leaves here, you know, antennas might be hiding in it, so let me sweep it.

166
00:15:07,065 --> 00:15:11,768
[SPEAKER_03]: But that doesn't mean now, for the rest of my time in the forest, I will walk with a broom everywhere.

167
00:15:12,587 --> 00:15:14,168
[SPEAKER_03]: No, if it looks good, I avoid it.

168
00:15:14,268 --> 00:15:15,409
[SPEAKER_03]: If it doesn't, it's all right.

169
00:15:15,969 --> 00:15:22,914
[SPEAKER_03]: But, if you start giving in, to know, I'll sweep this, and I'll sweep that, means you started acting out of fear and concern.

170
00:15:23,595 --> 00:15:27,998
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, you are losing perspective, which means, now you're even more exposed.

171
00:15:28,078 --> 00:15:40,027
[SPEAKER_03]: Basically, the more you act out towards defending what you obviously, implicitly took as valuable and yours, well, the wider the basis of the danger that you're applicable to becomes.

172
00:15:40,886 --> 00:15:47,047
[SPEAKER_03]: That's that monk that was in the arahant, in the suttas.

173
00:15:47,107 --> 00:15:48,268
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, people didn't know he was an arahant.

174
00:15:48,288 --> 00:15:51,148
[SPEAKER_03]: Going around the forest saying, oh, what a pleasure, what a joy, what a bliss.

175
00:15:51,769 --> 00:15:54,709
[SPEAKER_03]: And they thought, oh, he must be remembering his former wives or something.

176
00:15:54,789 --> 00:15:55,729
[SPEAKER_03]: So, they went and told the Buddha.

